## F4 — Angle brackets anywhere in prose are read as scaffold placeholders

**Severity: medium. Costs one round trip per occurrence.**

`design.md` was rejected with:

```
design 27 error Interfaces section contains scaffold placeholder text
```

The Interfaces section was fully written. The trigger was two fragments of
ordinary technical prose inside inline code spans:

```
`array<string>`
`<html data-theme>`
```

The placeholder detector matches `<...>` regardless of context, including inside
backticks. Both had to be reworded to "a list of strings" and "the `data-theme`
attribute of the root `html` element" — losing precision to satisfy a lint.

This is a real constraint for any technical design document. Generics, HTML
elements, type parameters, and shell placeholders all use angle brackets
naturally.

### Suggested fixes

1. Skip content inside backticks and fenced code blocks.
2. Match only the literal scaffold tokens the generator emits, rather than the
   general shape `<word>`.
3. Report the exact offending line. The finding pointed at line 27, the section
   heading area; the actual text was at lines 53 and 72. For a long section this
   is a search, not a fix.

